.\" Generated from crates/writ-tool/src/specs.rs; do not edit.
.TH WRIT\-VERIFY 1 2026-10-12 "writ 0.1.0" "writ manual"
.SH NAME
writ\-verify \- check a signature and what its signer may do
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nh
.ad l
\fBwrit verify\fR \fB\-\-as\fR\ \fIID\fR... \fB\-t\fR\ \fITAG\fR \fB\-c\fR\ \fICONTEXT\fR [\fB\-\-in\fR\ \fIPATH\fR]... [\fB\-\-at\fR\ \fITIME\fR] [\fB\-\-state\fR\ \fIDIR\fR] [\fB\-\-leaf\-ceiling\fR\ \fIDUR\fR] [\fB\-q\fR] \fIFILE\fR \fISIGNATURE\fR
.ad
.hy
.SH DESCRIPTION
Check that SIGNATURE signs FILE for CONTEXT, and that the signer holds TAG. If so, writ prints the chain of grants that shows it and exits with status 0. If not, it prints the reasons to standard error and exits with status 1.
.PP
writ reads statements, identity logs and revocations from the \-\-in files, from standard input when it is a file or a pipe, and from each \-\-as identity directory. The order does not matter.
.PP
writ uses the current time, but never a time before its own build date. With \-\-state, it also never uses a time before the newest statement it has seen from the \-\-as identities, so setting the clock back does not make expired grants valid again. Use \-\-at to ask about another time.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
\fIFILE\fR
the signed file; '\-' means standard input
.TP
\fISIGNATURE\fR
the signature file
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-as\fR \fIID\fR
the identity whose point of view to use: an id or an identity directory
.TP
\fB\-t\fR, \fB\-\-tag\fR \fITAG\fR
what the signer must hold, such as '(release)'
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-context\fR \fICONTEXT\fR
what the signature must be for
.TP
\fB\-\-in\fR \fIPATH\fR
read statements from PATH; '\-' means standard input
.TP
\fB\-\-at\fR \fITIME\fR
answer for TIME instead of now: 2027\-01\-01, 2027\-01\-01T12:00Z, or @SECONDS
.TP
\fB\-\-state\fR \fIDIR\fR
keep state in DIR between runs: the identity logs and revocations seen, and the clock floor
.TP
\fB\-\-leaf\-ceiling\fR \fIDUR\fR
accept grants to leaf keys only if they last at most DUR (at most 24h)
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
print nothing; only set the exit status
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
print help; \fB\-h\fR is short, \fB\-\-help\fR is long
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
success; for questions, the answer is yes
.TP
1
the answer is no: the check, verification, proof or name lookup was denied
.TP
2
an error: bad usage, unreadable input, or a failed operation
.SH EXAMPLES
Check a release:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
writ verify \-\-as me \-t '(release)' \-c example/release/v1 app.tar app.tar.sig < bundle.writ
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBwrit\fR(1), \fBwrit\-sign\fR(1), \fBwrit\-check\fR(1)
